<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Teutones</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">late 2nd century BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/2nd-century-bce">2nd century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Teutones were an ancient people, probably from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jutland">Jutland</a>, who migrated south with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cimbri">Cimbri</a> in the late 2nd century BCE. Together they defeated several Roman armies, most heavily at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orange-vaucluse">Arausio</a> in 105 BCE, before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaius-marius">Gaius Marius</a> destroyed the Teutones at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aix-en-provence">Aquae Sextiae</a> in 102 BCE. Their name later became a byword for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germanic-peoples">Germanic peoples</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/teutones.jpg" alt="Teutones" width="320" height="209" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Heinrich Leutemann (1824-1904). · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Die_Frauen_der_Teutonen_verteidigen_die_Wagenburg_by_Heinrich_Leutemann.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
